GOOGLE_API_KEY=your_google_maps_api_key_here

# ── Backend address ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# constants/api.ts resolves the base URL in this order:
#   1. An in-app override saved from the Server Settings screen (AsyncStorage)
#   2. EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL (inlined at build time)
#   3. Auto-detection from the Expo dev-server address (dev builds only)
#   4. http://localhost:8080 (emulator only)
#
# For LOCAL DEV: leave this unset. Step 3 detects your machine's current LAN
# IP from Constants.expoConfig.hostUri, and metro.config.js proxies /api/**
# through to the backend on localhost:8080. Only set this if the backend runs
# somewhere other than the machine running `expo start`.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.23:8080
#
# For EAS BUILDS this file does NOTHING. The repo root .gitignore has `**/.env`
# and EAS only uploads git-tracked files, so the variable would be undefined
# in the build. Set it in eas.json under build.<profile>.env instead — that is
# where the preview profile's URL lives. A standalone build has no Metro dev
# server and no proxy, so it must point at the backend (or a tunnel to it)
# directly, on the real port.
#
# To move the address after a build ships, use the in-app Server Settings
# screen (step 1) rather than rebuilding.

# ── Google sign-in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials. Full click-by-click walkthrough: docs/GOOGLE_SIGN_IN_SETUP.md
#
# Only TWO values go in the app, and which one matters depends on platform:
#   • WEB client ID — used on ANDROID. Not a typo: the Android OAuth client
#     authorises the app by package name + signing certificate, but the ID
#     token it returns is minted for the *web* client, so that's what the app
#     passes to configure() and what lands in the token's `aud` claim.
#   • iOS client ID — used on iOS, for both purposes.
# You still create an Android OAuth client in the console (Google won't issue
# a token without one), its ID just never appears here.
#
# These are public identifiers, not secrets — that's why they can be inlined
# into the app bundle. No client secret is ever used.
#
# The same IDs must also be set on the backend as GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_IDS
# (comma-separated, in the repo-root .env). That server-side list is what
# actually enforces that a Google token was issued for this app — without it,
# /api/auth/google rejects every sign-in.
#
# Same EAS caveat as EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL above: this file is not uploaded
# to EAS, so for a real build these belong in eas.json under build.<profile>.env.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
#
# iOS ALSO needs the reversed iOS client ID as a URL scheme. app.json can't
# carry a comment, hence this note — add the plugin config there once you have
# the ID (see docs/GOOGLE_SIGN_IN_SETUP.md step 6):
#
#   ["@react-native-google-signin/google-signin", {
#     "iosUrlScheme": "com.googleusercontent.apps.YOUR-IOS-CLIENT-ID-SUFFIX"
#   }]
#
# Google sign-in CANNOT run in Expo Go — it needs a development build. Phone,
# email and password sign-in all work in Expo Go as normal.
